{"metric":"bonnor_magnetic_dipole","source":[{"metric":"bonnor_magnetic_dipole","system":"spheroidal","fields":["coords","inverse_metric_components","metric_components","parameters"],"version":"0467756c71d77fa1"}],"views":[{"id":"spheroidal_axis","label":"The axis beyond a hole","box":[-3.4916,3.4916,-3.3916,3.3916],"system":"spheroidal","restriction":"The half axis $\\theta = 0$ beyond one hole only, totally geodesic, each point in the diagram a single event.","settings":"$m = 1$, the unit of every length, $b = 2\\sqrt{2}\\,m$, and $\\ell = 4\\,m$.","caption":["The half axis $\\theta = 0$ of Bonnor's magnetic dipole beyond one of its two black holes ($m = 1$, $b = 2\\sqrt{2}\\,m$), each point in the diagram a single event. The metric on it is $-(Z/Y)^2c^2dt^2 + (Y/Z)^2dr^2$, and with $r_* = \\int (Y/Z)^2dr$, which runs from $-\\infty$ at $r = 4m$ to $\\infty$, the maps $p = \\arctan((ct - r_*)/\\ell)$ and $q = \\arctan((ct + r_*)/\\ell)$ bring it into the whole diamond, drawn with $T = p + q$ up and $X = q - p$ across.","The two null edges on the left are $r = 4m$, the horizon of the hole, where $r_*$ runs off as the inverse of the distance to it, the mark of a degenerate horizon. Light reaches it as $t \\to \\pm\\infty$, after a finite affine distance, since $r$ is an affine parameter along every ray."],"layers":[{"kind":"fill","class":"region","points":[[3.1416,0.0],[0.0,3.1416],[-3.1416,0.0],[0.0,-3.1416]]},{"kind":"fill","class":"cover","points":[[3.1416,0.0],[0.0,3.1416],[-3.1416,0.0],[0.0,-3.1416]]},{"kind":"line","class":"r","points":[[0.0,-3.1396],[0.0028,-2.969],[0.0109,-2.8011],[0.0234,-2.6402],[0.0426,-2.4588],[0.0701,-2.2529],[0.1042,-2.037],[0.2575,-1.1949],[0.3202,-0.7951],[0.3467,-0.5735],[0.3632,-0.3858],[0.3732,-0.2053],[0.3772,-0.0087],[0.375,0.15],[0.3673,0.3226],[0.3539,0.4995],[0.3349,0.6797],[0.3072,0.8878],[0.2757,1.0882],[0.1157,1.9697],[0.0585,2.3353],[0.0328,2.5456],[0.0145,2.7493],[0.0035,2.9493],[0.0,3.1396]]},{"kind":"line","class":"r",